            "providerContent": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Alberto builds agents with a focus on measuring them before they ship and keeping a human in the loop where it matters.\n\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** The agent on his portfolio site records eval cases against a real model and replays them in CI with no model or network. A stale recording or an off-topic answer fails the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy, human in the loop:** His fraud detection agent recommends approve, reject or request-info, and a human admin decides. It only auto-approves on evidence verified in code, and web sources can hold a decision back but never approve it.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** The portfolio agent has a per-request token budget, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Each eval report shows mean tokens, latency and cost per case. ([guardrails](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public#guardrails))\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Every eval run appends its metrics to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show up against the previous run. ([eval history](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/history.jsonl))\n\nResults so far: on his fraud detection agent (31 offline cases), approved fraud fell from 54% to 8% over twelve iterations on a local 9B model. He chose Claude Haiku 5.5 for deployment, at 86% accuracy versus 71% and about $0.002 per review. Its stack is TypeScript, PostgreSQL and GCP."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
