              "type": "response_end",
              "response": {
                "text": "Alberto builds agents with a focus on measurement, human oversight and cost control:\n\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** Eval cases are recorded against a real model and replayed in CI with no network, so stale recordings or off-topic answers fail the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy with a human in the loop:** In his fraud detection agent, the agent recommends and a human admin decides. It auto-approves only on evidence verified in code, and web sources can hold a decision back but never approve it.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** His portfolio agent has a per-request token budget, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show mean tokens, latency and cost per case.\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run is appended to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show up against the previous run.\n\nFor example, on the fraud detection agent (offline evals, 31 cases), he moved from a local 9B model to Claude Haiku 5.5, which reached 86% accuracy versus 71% and let 1% of fraud through versus 8%, at about $0.002 per review. The stack for that project is TypeScript, PostgreSQL and GCP.",
